#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175685 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176038 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71acfb6e-910a-5b33-8a4c-45867c3d69c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Linear Combination of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "441d6816-7c83-52aa-b225-f2b108d4c906" :question/math-academy-id "e-10064" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(-1,3), \\qquad \nX_2 \\sim  N(3,2), \\qquad \nX_3 \\sim N(2,1)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y = X_1 - 2X_2 - X_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1), \\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nand $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1 \\sim N(-1,3), \\qquad \nX_2 \\sim  N(3,2), \\qquad \nX_3 \\sim N(2,1),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = X_1 - 2X_2 - X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-6-2 \\\\  & =-9, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3+8+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3} & ∼N(-9,12).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175686 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a579ddb-4754-5083-9bb2-e14e7bdfc19e" :knowledge-point/title "Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "4b936a95-2418-5350-bef1-e13867b85bb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10065" :question/problem "A shop sells $2$ types of rice, Type $A$ and Type $B.$ The volume per packet for Type $A$ rice is normally distributed with a mean of $1$ liter and a variance of $0.01$ liters2, while the volume per packet for Type $B$ rice is normally distributed with a mean of $0.7$ liters and a variance of $0.02$ liters2. During cooking, it is known that Type $A$ rice quadruples in volume, while Type $B$ rice triples in volume.\n\nIf a single package of Type $A$ rice and $2$ packages of Type $B$ rice are randomly selected and cooked, what is the probability that the resulting rice volume is less than $8$ liters? You may assume that the volumes of rice in the packages are mutually independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3594 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3974 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3936 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3897 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3859 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4364 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4325 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_1$ denote the volume of the Type $A$ rice package, and let $X_2$ and $X_3$ denote the volumes of the Type $B$ rice packages. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(1, 0.01), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(0.7, 0.02), \\qquad X_3\\sim N(0.7, 0.02).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the total resulting volume of rice after cooking. Since the Type $A$ rice quadruples in volume, while the type $B$ rice triples in volume, we have\n\n$$\nY= 4X_1+3X_2 + 3X_3.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =(4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0.7+3\\cdot 0.7) \\\\  & =8.2 \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={4}^{2}\\cdot 0.01+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.02+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0.01+9\\cdot 0.02+9\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =0.52.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(8.2, 0.52).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y \\lt 8),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<8) & =P(Z<\\frac{8-8.2}{\\sqrt{0.52}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-0.28) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-0.28)\\approx 0.3897.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<8) & ≈Φ(-0.28) \\\\  & ≈0.3897.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175865 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3414fb92-7c59-5e59-af67-5c57b75d759f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "6e7d897d-a59c-5944-b515-81132fd17715" :question/math-academy-id "e-9265" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(-1,2^2), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim  N(3, 3^2), \\qquad \nX_3\\sim N(4,2)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y = X_1 + X_2 + X_3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1),\\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2),\\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(-1,2^2), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim  N(3, 3^2), \\qquad \nX_3\\sim N(4,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY = X_1 + X_2 + X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-1+3+4 \\\\  & =6, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+{3}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =4+9+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(6,15).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc2565c7-5dcd-5573-9231-26c26cdae18c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Probabilities Involving Linear Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "722126ae-cdef-5c3f-a031-f4ccefdc6fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9266" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(4,5), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim N(-2,2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(3,1)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent and $Y=3X_1-3X_2-X_3,$ calculate $P(|Y| \\lt 3).$ \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.03 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.04 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.05 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0129 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0125 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0122 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0166 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0162 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0158 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -2.0 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0212 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0207 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0202 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.5 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0668 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0655 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0643 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.4 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0808 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0793 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0778 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -1.3 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0968 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0951 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0934 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1), \\qquad \nX_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(\\mu_3,\\sigma^2_3),\n$$\n\nand $a_1,a_2,a_3$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(4,5), \\qquad\nX_2\\sim N(-2,2), \\qquad\nX_3\\sim N(3,1),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=3X_1-3X_2-X_3.\n$$\n\nSince $X_1, X_2,$ and $X_3$ are mutually independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5+(-3)^{2}\\cdot 2+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5+9\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∼N(15,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|Y |\\lt  3)=P(-3 <  Y \\lt  3).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3<Y<3) & =P(\\frac{-3-15}{\\sqrt{64}}<Z<\\frac{3-15}{\\sqrt{64}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.25<Z<-1.5) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.5)-P(Z<-2.25) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.5)-Φ(-2.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.5)≈0.0668,\\,Φ(-2.25)≈0.0122.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|Y|<3) & =Φ(-1.5)-Φ(-2.25) \\\\  & ≈0.0668-0.0122 \\\\  & =0.0546.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176038 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71acfb6e-910a-5b33-8a4c-45867c3d69c3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Linear Combination of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e750ca1-0028-593b-b691-2d6aec16572e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(9,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497866} {:answer/id #uuid "44aad863-f96a-579d-b680-6ce5d56a02a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,72)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497951} {:answer/id #uuid "4d54ca23-c0cf-5217-bc85-5e3536134a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(9,70)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497978} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd6f88f-8b8d-5e32-9d78-e9dbd7ed4963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(9,73)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498257} {:answer/id #uuid "d6292a34-5400-590e-b4c1-749f608bf003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(9,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbd6f88f-8b8d-5e32-9d78-e9dbd7ed4963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(9,73)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498257} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aeedcef-fc68-5134-bce1-dbbbab9c2929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad7b27e3-8c78-5b01-8912-d95497958678" :question/math-academy-id "q-114458" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,3)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,4),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,3),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 4+{3}^{2}\\cdot 1+{4}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4+9\\cdot 1+16\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =73.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3} & ∼N(9,73).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176039 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a579ddb-4754-5083-9bb2-e14e7bdfc19e" :knowledge-point/title "Combinations of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8041b8be-8957-5a2c-846c-ea79936b4958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0272" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173993} {:answer/id #uuid "8e220953-d7e1-5e87-8c43-b3066bfd9c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1505" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173994} {:answer/id #uuid "97d525b7-7e77-5d0e-91ef-2632f7d5f49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1212" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173995} {:answer/id #uuid "a08990c7-9968-5dee-9bd6-9c1068336a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173996} {:answer/id #uuid "38620165-1626-58f3-a1d2-fde318ff74c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38620165-1626-58f3-a1d2-fde318ff74c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0207" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173997} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b327c0a-88c1-5e92-8e65-9c6e5d5bea32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "888af95f-b68b-5b6b-8b0f-fb590f801c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-114463" :question/problem "A course grade is calculated by adding the results of a written test, oral test, and colloquium, contributing $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%,$ respectively to the final grade. \n\nThe written test results are normally distributed with a mean of $64$ and a standard deviation of $8.$ The oral test results are normally distributed with a mean of $50$ and a standard deviation of $10$, while the results of the colloquium are normally distributed with a mean of $60$ and a standard deviation of $10.$ Find the probability that a randomly selected student achieves a grade greater than $70.$ You may assume that the scores on each test are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{1}$, ${X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ denote the written test, oral test, and colloquium results, respectively. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(64,{8}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(50,{10}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(60,{10}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ denote the final grade of a randomly selected student. Since the written, oral, and colloquium contribute $50%$, $30%,$ and $20%$, respectively, to the final grade, we have\n\n$$\nY=0.5{X}_{1}+0.3{X}_{2}+0.2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nThe random variable $Y$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =0.5\\cdot 64+0.3\\cdot 50+0.2\\cdot 60 \\\\  & =59, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & =(0.5)^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+(0.3)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2}+(0.2)^{2}\\cdot {10}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25\\cdot 64+0.09\\cdot 10+0.04\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ∼N(59,29).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(Y>70),$ we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & =P(Z>\\frac{70-59}{\\sqrt{29}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>2.04) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<2.04) \\\\  & =1-Φ(2.04)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.04)≈0.9793.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>70) & ≈1-Φ(2.04) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9793 \\\\  & =0.0207.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3414fb92-7c59-5e59-af67-5c57b75d759f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497856} {:answer/id #uuid "52512766-78b5-5354-a8d5-e0d1d50160cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497996} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bd4fc9-def5-513d-9ca7-7f8f3f8a2d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(15,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498201} {:answer/id #uuid "e43a496c-422e-5091-8a74-3dfa5e0aaf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(15,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498306} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d11e4f-b65d-5603-aa21-940c487b15e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1,36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1845867d-147d-53d3-94fd-485e7853e4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497856} :answer-field/id #uuid "6272a243-0d5a-50d0-8046-f6e45f838f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b666acc-0963-5776-a5f2-9fbed9a3deef" :question/math-academy-id "q-142935" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-7,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,3)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-7,1),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,3),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =3+(-7)+5 \\\\  & =1, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(1,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3414fb92-7c59-5e59-af67-5c57b75d759f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e2ab2a-41df-5082-adcc-efb6a5b40d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(30,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497885} {:answer/id #uuid "360cd63a-5942-576e-984b-d880c6a49efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497920} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dfca87-64c7-5892-a5e2-c989f947aac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498230} {:answer/id #uuid "c4294185-d9a4-51c5-b464-dd49e730abc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(30,21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498233} {:answer/id #uuid "e780bf2e-ab38-5d2a-a1d1-85bffcb47824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(8,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2dfca87-64c7-5892-a5e2-c989f947aac3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498230} :answer-field/id #uuid "60af626e-3ef7-5d7c-96e7-7dc36bbcb60d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2d92ca9-1354-51b9-b571-e879ca411a35" :question/math-academy-id "q-142938" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-2,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,4),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,2)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(-2,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-1,4),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(5,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =-2+(-1)+5 \\\\  & =2, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(2,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176012 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3414fb92-7c59-5e59-af67-5c57b75d759f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474c014b-6c51-580e-8f35-4173a8bdc5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(2,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497962} {:answer/id #uuid "75ccee7a-ee30-58cb-9659-bc3ed84a9f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(4,49)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498080} {:answer/id #uuid "957ed374-2949-53b9-a263-375ab1c354f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498139} {:answer/id #uuid "af78e032-61cc-5c78-97ac-8a372331d0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(4,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498189} {:answer/id #uuid "cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(4,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab08509-d2eb-5a2b-976d-8632d673be4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(4,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498249} :answer-field/id #uuid "277c902d-e34f-5883-b47e-dce978610a9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6053da2-f1d5-5511-b304-aa8032f24e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-142941" :question/problem "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,{3}^{2})\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3},\\,{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(0,{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2}+{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =1+3+0 \\\\  & =4, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+2+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =4+2+9 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+{X}_{3} & ∼N(4,15).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218617